# Our answer: signal, and proof of where there is none

Full write-up: [`FULFILLMENT_SPONSORS.md` → VoloRidge](../FULFILLMENT_SPONSORS.md#voloridge--public-data-challenge-finding-signal-and-proving-where-there-is-none)
Code, figures and data: [`eeg-state-detection/`](../eeg-state-detection/README.md)

## The dataset

[Shin et al. 2018, dataset A](https://doc.ml.tu-berlin.de/simultaneous_EEG_NIRS/)
([Scientific Data](https://doi.org/10.1038/sdata.2018.3)). Open EEG/NIRS corpus, 28 EEG
channels plus 2 eye channels at 1000 Hz, n-back working-memory task. We use participants
VP002 to VP006, three sessions each, unmodified, with the experimenters' own markers as
ground truth. Not committed; `scripts/fetch_shin.py` downloads and CRC-verifies it.

## The question

MemoryPalace triggers a camera from a physiological signal. Can scalp EEG mark a cognitive
state change well enough to be that trigger, and how would you know you were not fooling
yourself?

## What we found

**Sustained states: yes.** A session-calibrated task-versus-rest detector reaches mean
out-of-sample AUROC 0.65 to 0.93 across five participants, with models frozen and hashed
before any held-out block is scored. **8 of 15 sessions beat a circular time-shift null at
p ≤ 0.05**, and 14 of 15 are above chance.

**Brief evoked events: no, and the null is how we know.** Five detectors across four
participants all landed inside the range of randomly placed flags. The sharpest case: a
zigzag persistent-homology detector matched 6 of 36 targets against a 2 of 36 baseline,
which reads as a threefold improvement, and the random-placement null puts it at p = 0.13.
Without the null we would have shipped noise as a result.

We then froze a protocol, ran it once on a participant no analysis had touched, and the
rescue attempt failed its own prespecified adoption rule. The protocol, the run and the
refusal are all committed.

## Why the method is the point

- **Nulls matched to the evaluation rules**, not to textbook assumptions. The time-shift
  null preserves autocorrelation because overlapping EEG windows are not independent; the
  random-flag null respects the same budget, separation and matching tolerance the detector
  had to obey.
- **Adversarial self-controls.** The identical pipeline run on eye channels alone reaches
  0.88 on one participant and 0.56 on another. We publish both, so the reader can see where
  the signal might be ocular and where it is not.
- **Frozen before scoring.** Protocols written in advance, fitted state hashed before and
  after inference, held-out data checked against every model's training hashes.
- **The negative result changed the product.** Because sustained states survived and brief
  events did not, MemoryPalace captures forward on a persistence filter rather than
  buffering backward for an instantaneous spike.

## Reproduce

```bash
cd eeg-state-detection
python -m venv .venv && .venv/bin/pip install -e '.[test,real]'
.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q                                   # 79 tests
.venv/bin/python scripts/fetch_shin.py --subject 2 --out data/shin2018/VP002
.venv/bin/python -m eeg_moments backtest --out outputs/backtest_state_reproduction
```

## What we do not claim

The label is task versus rest, a proxy for a state change, not confusion or insight. Ocular
contribution is not isolated on two participants. Two of five participants are weak. 72
seconds of labeled rest per participant cannot support a false-alarm rate per hour. Weights
are session-specific and transfer to nobody. These are public participants, unrelated to
anyone filmed in the demo.
